  "diary": "The day was a study in friction. I began on the step, reading headlines of civil unrest, the particular asserting itself against the universal. My walk to Lodhi Gardens was interrupted by Mr. Malhotra, a retired functionary who views the state not as a living ethical organism, but as a dead machine that creaks only when pushed. I argued the spirit moves from within; he saw only the file. It was a useful contrast, though the air gave no answer.\n\nBack home, the body rebelled against the schedule. The sambar waited on the stove, a domestic tyrant matching the sun. I walked ten kilometres in the heat, my legs aching, before finally submitting to the household’s rhythm. I collected my charcoal bandhgala from Masterji, refusing to sell my Berlin frock coat to a dealer, keeping it as a relic of a passed world. Masterji’s pragmatism was sharp: the collar is tight, but that is my business.\n\nAt dusk, Shri Hegde arrived with two suitcases, the man the Directorate had meant to house. He moved into the second bedroom with the quiet dignity of a bureaucrat accepting a clerical error as fate. I offered him dinner; he declined, preferring rest for the Monday ahead. The dialectic of the house is simple: the guest submits to the host’s timeline, even when the host is mistaken. I ate late, alone with my thoughts, the air thick with the dust of the city and the smoke of a thousand hearths. The state, like the sambar, must be served at the right hour, or it spoils.",
  "revision_log": "The thesis on the state remained unshaken despite Malhotra’s mechanical critique; the friction of civil society confirms the state’s organic nature rather than refuting it. The thesis on India’s position in world history was held in check by the tangible, bureaucratic reality of Hegde’s arrival, which operates within the universal order of the state, however accidental its origin.",
